  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Re-Imagining Class is already showing signs of use, with 1,277 recorded downloads and open availability through a repository instance. Its record also points to clear policy and teaching relevance through alignment with the SDG “Reduced inequalities” and a funding acknowledgment from KU Leuven.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2024 by Leuven University Press, Re-Imagining Class addresses class through the connected lenses of precarity, intersectionality, and cultural analysis. The book’s framing places literature, theatre, and film at the center of contemporary debates about social transformation, with contributors drawing on examples across several national and linguistic contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact at this stage is use rather than citation. The book has been downloaded 1,277 times in OAPEN, with activity spread across the year rather than confined to a single burst. That pattern suggests sustained reader interest and early uptake in open-access circulation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also shows open repository availability, which can broaden access for researchers, students, and practitioners. In addition, the book’s SDG alignment with “Reduced inequalities” indicates relevance to policy and public-interest conversations about social stratification, vulnerability, and inclusion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">By contrast, the citation record is still nascent: OpenAlex currently reports no citations. That does not diminish the book’s intellectual ambition; it simply means scholarly reception is still in its early phase. On the available evidence, Re-Imagining Class is positioned as an accessible, interdisciplinary contribution whose immediate reach is being expressed through downloads, open access, and thematic relevance rather than citation accumulation.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}
